      "heading": "BPE: merge the most frequent pair, repeat",
      "narration": "Byte-Pair Encoding is one of those algorithms that is so simple you suspect you have misunderstood it. You have not. Here it is in full. Start by treating every single character as its own token, so your vocabulary is just the alphabet. Now go through your entire training corpus and count every adjacent pair of tokens — how many times does t followed by h appear, how many times does h followed by e appear, and so on for every pair. Find the most frequent pair. Merge it: create one new token that means that pair, add it to the vocabulary, and replace every occurrence in the corpus. Then count all the pairs again, in the corpus you have just rewritten, and merge the new most frequent pair. Repeat until your vocabulary has as many entries as you asked for. That is it. That is the whole algorithm, and you will implement it in the lab in about thirty lines. Watch what happens as it runs. The first merges are the most common letter pairs — t h, e r, i n. Then those merged tokens start appearing in pairs themselves, so t-h merges with e to give the whole word the. A few thousand merges in, every common English word is a single token, and the pieces left over are exactly the productive fragments: ing, ed, un, tion, pre. Nobody taught it morphology. It has never heard of a suffix. It simply merged what was frequent, and frequency in real language happens to track meaning, because the pieces people reuse are the pieces that mean something. Now the part that trips people up. The output of training is not really a vocabulary; it is an ordered list of merge rules. To tokenize a new piece of text, you split it into characters and then replay those merges in exactly the order they were learned. Order matters — apply them in a different order and you get different tokens. Which is why the tokenizer is a real artifact that ships alongside the weights, and why using the wrong tokenizer with a model produces fluent nonsense rather than an error. Two consequences worth naming. First, the vocabulary reflects your corpus, warts and all. Train it on English web text and English gets efficient tokens while Nepali or Thai gets chopped into bytes, so the same sentence costs three or four times as many tokens in one language as another — which is a real cost difference and a real quality difference. Second, the modern refinement: byte-level BPE, which GPT-2 introduced and almost everything since has used. Instead of starting from characters, start from the two hundred and fifty-six possible bytes. Now literally any input is representable — any script, any emoji, any corrupted encoding, any binary blob — and the unknown token stops existing altogether. It is a small change and it removed an entire category of bug.",
